Step 1: Install Docker

Command

sudo apt update && sudo apt install docker.io -y

One-line description: Updates Ubuntu packages and installs Docker.

Expected output

Reading package lists... Done
...
Setting up docker.io ...
Processing triggers ...
Step 2: Start and verify Docker

Command

sudo systemctl enable --now docker && docker --version

One-line description: Starts Docker automatically and displays the installed Docker version.

Expected output

Docker version 28.x.x, build xxxxxxx

Your exact version may be different. That's completely fine.

Step 3: Install kubectl and Minikube
Install kubectl
sudo snap install kubectl --classic

Description: Installs the Kubernetes command-line tool.

Output

kubectl 1.xx.x from Canonical installed

Verify:

kubectl version --client

Output

Client Version: v1.xx.x
Install Minikube
curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64 && sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube

Description: Downloads and installs Minikube.

Verify:

minikube version

Output

minikube version: v1.xx.x
Step 4: Start Kubernetes
minikube start --driver=docker

One-line description: Creates and starts a Kubernetes cluster using Docker.

Expected output

😄  minikube v1.xx.x on Ubuntu
✨  Using the docker driver
📌  Using Docker driver
🚀  Starting control plane node
🌟  Enabled addons: storage-provisioner, default-storageclass
🏄  Done! kubectl is now configured to use "minikube" cluster
If you get Docker permission error

Run:

sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

Description: Gives your Ubuntu user permission to use Docker without sudo.

Then log out and log in again, and run:

minikube start --driver=docker
Step 5: Create the project
mkdir ~/k8s-docker && cd ~/k8s-docker

One-line description: Creates the project folder and enters it.

Output

No output
Step 6: Create the HTML application

Instead of opening nano, use this single command:

echo '<h1>Hello from Docker and Kubernetes!</h1><p>DevOps Lab Experiment</p>' > index.html

One-line description: Creates a simple HTML webpage.

Output

No output

Check it:

cat index.html

Output

<h1>Hello from Docker and Kubernetes!</h1><p>DevOps Lab Experiment</p>
Step 7: Create Dockerfile

Use this single command:

cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EXPOSE 80
EOF

One-line description: Creates a Dockerfile that runs the HTML page using Nginx.

Output

No output

Check:

cat Dockerfile

Output

FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EXPOSE 80
Step 8: Build the Docker image
minikube image build -t myapp:v1 .

One-line description: Builds the Docker image directly inside Minikube.

Expected output

[+] Building ...
 => [internal] load build definition from Dockerfile
 => [internal] load metadata for docker.io/library/nginx:alpine
 => [1/2] FROM docker.io/library/nginx:alpine
 => [2/2] COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
 => exporting to image
 => naming to docker.io/library/myapp:v1
Step 9: Create Kubernetes Deployment

Use this single command:

cat > deployment.yaml <<'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: myapp
        image: myapp:v1
        imagePullPolicy: Never
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
EOF

One-line description: Defines two Kubernetes Pods that run the Docker image.

Output

No output
Step 10: Deploy the application
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

One-line description: Creates the Kubernetes Deployment.

Output

deployment.apps/myapp created

Verify:

kubectl get deployments

Output

NAME    READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
myapp   2/2     2            2           10s
Step 11: Create and expose the Service

Again, use one command:

cat > service.yaml <<'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: myapp-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: myapp
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
    nodePort: 30080
EOF

One-line description: Creates a NodePort Service to expose the application.

Output

No output

Apply it:

kubectl apply -f service.yaml

One-line description: Creates the Kubernetes Service.

Output

service/myapp-service created
Step 12: Test the application

This is the most important command for your lab.

curl http://$(minikube ip):30080

One-line description: Sends a request to the application through the Kubernetes Service.

Output

<h1>Hello from Docker and Kubernetes!</h1><p>DevOps Lab Experiment</p>

If you see this, your Docker + Kubernetes integration is working. ✅

Step 13: Final verification

Instead of checking everything separately, remember this:

kubectl get pods,deployments,svc

One-line description: Displays the Pods, Deployment and Service status together.

Expected output

NAME                         READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
pod/myapp-xxxxxxxxxx-xxxxx  1/1     Running   0          1m
pod/myapp-xxxxxxxxxx-xxxxx  1/1     Running   0          1m

NAME                    READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
deployment.apps/myapp   2/2     2            2           1m

NAME                   TYPE       CLUSTER-IP      EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)
service/myapp-service  NodePort   10.xxx.xxx.xxx  <none>        80:30080/TCP
Step 14: Cleanup
kubectl delete -f service.yaml && kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml && minikube stop

One-line description: Deletes the Kubernetes resources and stops Minikube.

Output

service "myapp-service" deleted
deployment.apps "myapp" deleted
✋  Stopping node "minikube" ...
🛑  1 node stopped.



STEPS 
Step 1: Install Docker
sudo apt update && sudo apt install docker.io -y

Step 2: Start Docker
sudo systemctl enable --now docker && docker --version

Step 3: Install kubectl
sudo snap install kubectl --classic

Verify:
kubectl version --client

Step 4: Install Minikube
curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64 && sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube

Verify:
minikube version

Step 5: Start Kubernetes
minikube start --driver=docker

If Docker permission error:
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

Log out and log in again, then:
minikube start --driver=docker

Step 6: Create project folder
mkdir ~/k8s-docker && cd ~/k8s-docker

Step 7: Create HTML file
echo '<h1>Hello from Docker and Kubernetes!</h1><p>DevOps Lab Experiment</p>' > index.html

Step 8: Create Dockerfile
cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EXPOSE 80
EOF

Step 9: Build Docker image
minikube image build -t myapp:v1 .

Step 10: Create Kubernetes Deployment
cat > deployment.yaml <<'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: myapp
        image: myapp:v1
        imagePullPolicy: Never
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
EOF

Step 11: Deploy application
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Verify:
kubectl get deployments

Step 12: Create Kubernetes Service
cat > service.yaml <<'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: myapp-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: myapp
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
    nodePort: 30080
EOF

Step 13: Apply Service
kubectl apply -f service.yaml

Step 14: Test application
curl http://$(minikube ip):30080

Step 15: Final verification
kubectl get pods,deployments,svc

Step 16: Cleanup
kubectl delete -f service.yaml && kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml && minikube stop
